{
  "summary": "This scan found one serious item and one minor one. A program is set to run automatically every time you sign in, and it uses a hidden window and a scrambled (encoded) PowerShell command, two tricks malware often uses. Look at that startup entry first. After that, confirm you recognize the extra <account-2> account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Hidden, encoded PowerShell command set to run at sign-in",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:b6d0057c"
      ],
      "explanation": "A startup entry named \"WindowsUpdateAssist\" is set to run automatically whenever your user account signs in. It starts PowerShell, a built-in Windows tool that can run any instructions, with its window hidden and its instructions scrambled (encoded) so you can't read them at a glance. The name sounds official, but the name alone proves nothing. What matters is the hidden window combined with the encoded command, which is a pattern malware often uses to run quietly and avoid notice. If you or a program you trust didn't set this up, treat it as a likely sign of unwanted software. Follow the fix shown for this finding in the report, and consider running a full scan with your security software."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Extra <account-2> account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, that has full control over the computer. This is often normal. Many people keep a separate <account-2> account on purpose, which is good practice. If you recognize it, there's nothing to do. If you don't, it could mean someone else gained access. Because the scan also found a suspicious startup entry, it's worth checking this account carefully rather than assuming it's fine. The report shows what to do for this finding."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
